One module, two bindings (`header` and `bar`) carrying the *identical*
interpolated declaration `background-color: $(Color.Background.Alt.box)`.

Each block gets its own class and its own variable: the variable seed is the
block class, which hashes the module, scope, binding and content. Each rule
reads only the variable its own `CSS.make` sets, and merging the two blocks
leaves the sheet order to decide, as for any two blocks.

  $ refmt --parse re --print ml input.re > output.ml
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl output.ml -o output.ml
  $ styled-ppx.generate output.ml > styles.css

The same declaration body gives two classes and two variables:

  $ cat styles.css
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @property --1cwxta0-0{syntax:"*";inherits:false;}
  @property --tcyq3p-0{syntax:"*";inherits:false;}
  .css-1cwxta0{background-color:var(--1cwxta0-0);}
  .css-tcyq3p{background-color:var(--tcyq3p-0);}
